{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"unique-ptr","title":"Smart Pointers: unique_ptr","group":"Copy, Move & Smart Pointers","order":35,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>std::unique_ptr&lt;T&gt;</code> is the sole owner of a heap object and deletes it when it goes out of scope. It's move-only, has zero overhead over a raw pointer, and should be your default for heap objects.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nstruct Widget {\n    std::string name;\n    explicit Widget(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"make \" &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n    ~Widget() { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"free \" &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    auto a = std::make_unique&lt;Widget&gt;(\"a\");       // preferred way to create\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a-&gt;name &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (*a).name &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::unique_ptr&lt;Widget&gt; b = std::move(a);     // ownership moves; copying is a compile error\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (a == nullptr) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b-&gt;name &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    b.reset(new Widget(\"c\"));                     // frees \"a\", now owns \"c\"\n    Widget* raw = b.get();                        // non-owning peek\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; raw-&gt;name &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"leaving main\\n\";\n}                                                  // \"c\" freed automatically</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>make a\na a\n1 a\nmake c\nfree a\nc\nleaving main\nfree c</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::make_unique&lt;T&gt;(args...)</code></td><td>Allocate and construct (exception-safe)</td></tr><tr><td><code>p.get()</code></td><td>Raw pointer, ownership kept</td></tr><tr><td><code>p.release()</code></td><td>Give up ownership — returns the raw pointer (you must delete it)</td></tr><tr><td><code>p.reset(q)</code></td><td>Delete current object, take ownership of <code>q</code> (or none)</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::move(p)</code></td><td>Transfer ownership</td></tr><tr><td><code>if (p)</code></td><td>Is it non-null?</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::make_unique&lt;T[]&gt;(n)</code></td><td>Array form — uses <code>delete[]</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Ownership in function signatures</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Node { int value; };\n\nstd::unique_ptr&lt;Node&gt; create(int v) { return std::make_unique&lt;Node&gt;(v); }  // returns ownership\nvoid inspect(const Node&amp; n) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"value \" &lt;&lt; n.value &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }  // just uses it\nvoid store(std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Node&gt;&gt;&amp; pool, std::unique_ptr&lt;Node&gt; n) {  // takes ownership\n    pool.push_back(std::move(n));\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Node&gt;&gt; pool;\n    auto n = create(7);\n    inspect(*n);\n    store(pool, std::move(n));\n    store(pool, create(9));\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; pool.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; pool[1]-&gt;value &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (n == nullptr) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>value 7\n2 9 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Guideline</div><p>Pass <code>unique_ptr</code> by value only to <em>transfer</em> ownership. A function that just uses the object should take <code>T&amp;</code>, <code>const T&amp;</code> or <code>T*</code> — it shouldn't care how the object is owned.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>Custom deleters</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstdio&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    auto closer = [](std::FILE* f) { std::fclose(f); std::cout &lt;&lt; \"closed\\n\"; };\n    {\n        std::unique_ptr&lt;std::FILE, decltype(closer)&gt; file(std::fopen(\"up_demo.txt\", \"w\"), closer);\n        std::fputs(\"data\", file.get());\n    }\n    std::remove(\"up_demo.txt\");\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>closed</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Polymorphism &amp; the pimpl idiom</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Shape { virtual ~Shape() = default; virtual double area() const = 0; };\nstruct Square : Shape { double s; explicit Square(double x) : s(x) {} double area() const override { return s * s; } };\nstruct Circle : Shape { double r; explicit Circle(double x) : r(x) {} double area() const override { return 3.0 * r * r; } };\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Shape&gt;&gt; shapes;\n    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;Square&gt;(2));\n    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;Circle&gt;(1));\n    double total = 0;\n    for (const auto&amp; s : shapes) total += s-&gt;area();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; total &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why prefer make_unique over unique_ptr&lt;T&gt;(new T)?</summary><div><p>It's shorter, never mentions <code>new</code>, and is exception-safe in expressions like <code>f(std::unique_ptr&lt;A&gt;(new A), g())</code> (before C++17 the <code>new A</code> could run, then <code>g()</code> throw, leaking A). It also keeps the “no naked new” rule easy to check.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the overhead of unique_ptr?</summary><div><p>None in practice: with the default (stateless) deleter it is exactly one pointer in size, and <code>get()</code>, <code>*</code> and <code>-&gt;</code> compile to the same instructions as a raw pointer. The destructor just calls <code>delete</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}